TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "player absence and offensive reorganization",
    "initiation profile similarity among teammates",
    "primary play types of absent player",
    "largest gainer in absent player's primary types",
    "pre-absence initiation profile (cosine similarity, Euclidean distance)",
    "substitute teammate metrics (minutes, initiations, same-position minutes)"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to measure 'closest pre-absence initiation profile' between teammates?",
    "How to define 'primary types' for an absent player?",
    "How to identify and measure the 'largest gainer' among teammates?",
    "How to handle small sample (n=148 spells, only 11 cases)?",
    "How to control for confounding by position and direct substitution role?",
    "How to avoid regression-to-mean artifacts when selecting on pre-absence similarity?",
    "How to ensure no lookahead when splitting pre-absence and during-absence periods?",
    "What statistical power is achievable with 11 cases?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No prior project evidence on this question was available in retrieval."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether the closest-profile teammate is the largest gainer.",
    "Whether minutes, initiations, or same-position minutes are better predictors.",
    "The typical magnitude and variance of initiation redistribution during absences.",
    "Any methodological benchmarks for measuring initiation profile similarity in this context."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question is a test of predictive discrimination: which of several pre-absence metrics best identifies the teammate whose initiations in the absent player's primary types increase most.",
    "With only 11 cases (likely 11 absence spells meeting minimum criteria), any estimator will have extremely wide confidence intervals; useful statistical power is implausible.",
    "The definition of 'closest pre-absence initiation profile' is ambiguous and requires a distance metric (cosine similarity, Euclidean, Jensen-Shannon) and a choice of feature space (play-type proportions, per-100-possessions rates).",
    "Defining 'primary types' requires a threshold that will be data-driven; few players have large enough initiation volumes to robustly estimate a play-type profile.",
    "The teammate who is the largest gainer may simply be the direct positional substitute, making the initiation-profile metric redundant with same-position minutes.",
    "Regression to the mean is a serious threat: a teammate whose pre-absence profile was slightly below their true mean may appear to gain simply by reverting to their mean, not because they are closest to the absent player's profile."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "What exactly '11 cases' refers to: unique players who missed games, team-absence combinations, or spells of a minimum length.",
    "The minimum absence length required for initiation profiles to meaningfully shift.",
    "The typical number of teammates per spell that compete to be the 'largest gainer'.",
    "The expected effect size (difference in gain between closest-profile teammate and next-closest)."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No prior records were retrieved; the decision rests solely on methodological assessment of the research question and the available data."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.05,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Only 11 cases (likely team-player-absence spells of qualifying length) are available. Any test of which teammate metric best predicts the largest gainer would be severely underpowered; even a large effect would be indistinguishable from noise."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "The number of spells where the absent player has a well-defined primary play type and there is a teammate with a meaningfully similar initiation profile is likely a small fraction of the 11 cases."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Initiation profiles are noisy estimates from limited possessions; play-type classification has known error rates. Measuring similarity between two noisy profile vectors (absent player and teammate) compounds the unreliability."
      },
      {
        "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
        "text": "Testing multiple proximity metrics (closest profile, most minutes, most initiations, same-position minutes) without pre-specification inflates false-positive risk."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No earlier study-family records were returned by retrieval, so we have no project-internal prior work to build on for this precise question."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "11 cases is far below what would be needed to detect a meaningful difference in predictive accuracy among competing metrics, or even to estimate the gain for a single metric with useful precision."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Many absence spells may lack a clear 'primary type' for the absent player, or may lack a teammate with enough pre-absence initiations in those types to measure a gain."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Initiation profiles are derived from play-by-play classification, which has non-trivial error rates. Small-sample profile estimates for individual players add noise that attenuates any true signal."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Selecting the teammate whose pre-absence profile is closest to the absent player's may capture a teammate who happened to be below their own true mean, so their 'gain' partly reflects regression to their mean rather than a causal substitution effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "Defining the absent player's 'primary types' from the same data used to measure the gain creates a risk of circularity and overfitting."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "The teammate who gains the most initiations is likely the direct positional replacement. Same-position minutes may be a confounder rather than an alternative metric; profile similarity may add no independent predictive value once position is controlled."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "The question compares multiple proximity definitions without pre-specification of a primary metric or a correction for multiple comparisons."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "If 'pre-absence' is not strictly defined relative to each absence spell's start date, during-absence data could contaminate the profile estimates."
    },
    {
      "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
      "text": "A teammate who already initiates a large share of a play type may have limited room to increase, biasing gain estimates downward for high-initiating teammates."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.02,
    "rejected": 0.02,
    "untestable": 0.92,
    "artifact": 0.03,
    "uncertain": 0.01
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "For each qualifying player-absence spell, the difference in the increase (during-absence minus pre-absence) of initiation volume in the absent player's primary play types between (a) the teammate whose pre-absence initiation profile has the highest cosine similarity to the absent player's profile and (b) all other teammates who played both pre- and during-absence. The estimand is the mean of this difference across spells, compared with analogous differences computed using alternative pre-absence teammate metrics (total minutes, total initiations, same-position minutes).",
    "unit": "One absence spell of a player missing at least 10 consecutive regular-season games, with complete play-by-play data for at least 20 pre-absence and 5 during-absence team games.",
    "sample": "All such spells from the 2022-23 through 2025-26 NBA seasons that yield at least one teammate with at least 50 pre-absence initiations and at least 20 pre-absence initiations in the absent player's primary types. Given the 11 cases reported, this may be zero after filtering; the minimum viable test would require pooling multiple additional seasons or leagues.",
    "power": "With 11 cases, even a large effect size (d = 0.8) would have power near 0.3 at alpha = 0.05 in a simple paired test. To reach 0.80 power for a medium effect (d = 0.5) would require approximately 50-60 spells. The available data cannot achieve useful power.",
    "controls": "Direct positional replacement status (same listed position), pre-absence on-court co-occurrence with the absent player, and pre-absence initiation volume in the primary play types, included as covariates in a hierarchical model.",
    "falsification": "Apply the same metric-selection procedure to placebo 'absences' of players who actually played (random game gaps), where no systematic gain should be attributed to profile similarity; if the metric still appears predictive, the method is picking up noise.",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-register a single primary definition of profile similarity (cosine similarity of play-type proportion vectors) as the predictor of interest, and treat the alternative metrics as secondary or exploratory. Use a hierarchical Bayesian model that shares information across spells and incorporates uncertainty in profile estimates.",
    "decision_rule": "If the posterior probability that the closest-profile teammate's gain exceeds the average gain of other teammates is greater than 0.95, declare the metric predictive. For comparisons between metrics, report Bayes factors without dichotomous decisions given the small n.",
    "data_version": "NBA play-by-play 2022-23 through 2025-26 seasons as of the 2026-11-01 cutoff, with play-type possessions from the project graph database release current at that date."
  }
}}

